course: dictionary
lesson: 1795
title: Clog: a shoe or a blockage
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so context tells you which one is right. «Mum put on her wooden clogs to walk in the garden.» Here a clog is a heavy shoe with a thick wooden sole.
explain: «Hair clogged the drain.» Here clog is a verb, and it means blocked so that water cannot pass through. Try the swap: "Hair blocked the drain" still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Look at the words around the verb. In the hagfish story, the slime «clogs an attacker's gills», so the clue is the attacker's gills, which need to let water through to breathe. Clog means blocking, not wearing shoes.
fact: A clog is a heavy shoe with a thick wooden sole.
fact: To clog something means to block it so things cannot pass through.
fact: «Hair clogged the drain.» Hair blocks the drain, so the water cannot flow away.
fact: «Popped his clogs» means died; it is humorous British English, a joke.
fact: Clogs are traditional shoes in the Netherlands and parts of England.
exercise: «Mum put on her wooden clogs to walk in the garden.» What is a clog here? | *A heavy shoe with a thick wooden sole | A type of soup | A bird that sings at dawn | A small wooden box
exercise: «Hair clogged the drain.» What does clogged mean? | Filled with warm water | Hung down loosely | Cleaned and polished until it shines | *Blocked, so water cannot pass through
exercise: In the hagfish story, what does the slime do to an attacker's gills? | Makes them shine | Cleans them with a brush | Warms them up | *Clogs them, so they cannot work properly
test: What does «popped his clogs» mean? | Won a prize | Lost his shoes | Went to sleep | *Died, in a humorous British way
test: Traffic can clog the roads at rush hour. What does clog mean here? | To wear wooden shoes | *To block so that things cannot move through | To shine brightly | To cook slowly
exam: Which fact about clogs is true? | Clogs are only worn in space | Clogs are a kind of fish | *Clogs are traditional shoes in the Netherlands and parts of England | Clogs are always made of cloth
